      "answer": "When you run a program twice, the second run is often faster\u2014not because your code improved, but because the system already did expensive work.\n\n**Compiled code caches:** Languages like Java and Python (via .pyc files) compile source to bytecode. The first run compiles and saves it; the second run reuses that file, skipping compilation.\n\n**File caches:** Operating systems keep recently read files in RAM. The first run reads your program, libraries, and data from disk. The second run finds them in RAM, which is much faster.\n\n**Data already in memory:** Databases, caches (like Redis), and even the OS keep hot data in memory. A second run may hit this warm cache instead of re-fetching from disk or network.\n\n**Why this matters:** If you change code and run it, and it runs faster, you can't tell whether the improvement came from your change or from warming caches. The first run may have been slow due to cold-start costs.\n\n**A fair comparison:** Run each version multiple times, discard the first (warm-up) run, and compare the average of the remaining runs. This isolates your code's actual effect from cache effects.",
