      "answer": "EXPERT STORE REVIEW\n\n1. Scope and intent\n\nThis module implements a MoE expert-weight loader for a trellis-style quantized model (up1 manifest, K=3/K=4 packed 1-bit experts, expert 256 being a shared expert). It supports two modes: a bounded LRU cache of per-expert slices, and a fully resident mode that keeps all experts in memory. The code is well-organized but has several correctness and performance hazards that should be addressed before production use.\n\n2. Performance risks\n\n2.1 The cache is a per-call miss amplifier. The `ExpertLayer.__call__` path performs `np.unique` on every request, then `store._get(self, int(expert))` per expert. Each `_get` miss triggers a synchronous `load_slice` from disk plus `mx.eval(arrays)` \u2014 a blocking host-device synchronization. On a cold cache with a 397B model, a single prefill with 256 experts per layer will stall on hundreds of sequential disk reads per layer. This is the dominant cost of the whole pipeline. Mitigations: prefetch the next batch's expert IDs asynchronously, batch the three slices per expert into one read, and avoid `mx.eval` on the loading path (the eval is only needed to pin the buffer; lazy evaluation lets MLX schedule the copy alongside compute).\n\n2.2 `mx.stack` per call is a full copy. In the non-resident path, `packed[projection]` is built by stacking the selected experts' slices into a new contiguous tensor. That is a full device copy of the selected weights (3 projections \u00d7 selected experts \u00d7 expert_bytes) on every single call. For a 397B model with large expert counts, this is a large copy of the selected weights on every call. The `expert_linear` kernel should accept a list of slices or a pointer table instead of forcing a contiguous copy. Alternatively, use `mx.concatenate` with a preallocated buffer, or better, keep the packed slices in a single contiguous buffer and index them.\n\n2.3 Cache thrashing. The cache is keyed by `(layer, expert)` and uses LRU with `popitem(last=False)`. If the working set of experts exceeds the cache limit, the cache will thrash \u2014 evicting experts that were just loaded, then re-reading them on the next call. This is",
